Under the **Anti‑Terrorism Act, 1997**, **Section 6 – Terrorism** defines terrorism as follows:

**Section 6(1) – Definition**  
“terrorism” means the use or threat of action where:  

(a) the action falls within the meaning of sub‑section (2), and  

(b) the use or threat is designed to coerce and intimidate or overawe the Government, the public or a section of the public, a foreign government or population, an international organization, or to create a sense of fear or insecurity in society;  

(c) the use or threat is made for the purpose of advancing a religious, sectarian or ethnic cause.  

A proviso states that nothing in this provision applies to a democratic and religious rally or a peaceful demonstration in accordance with law. [Source 1]

**Section 6(2) – Acts that constitute “action”**  
An “action” falls within sub‑section (1) if it involves any of the following:  

(a) doing anything that causes death;  
(b) grievous violence against a person or grievous bodily injury or harm to a person;  
(c) grievous damage to property, including government premises, official installations, schools, hospitals, offices or any other public or private property, including damage by ransacking, looting or arson;  
(d) doing anything likely to cause death or endangering a person’s life;  
(e) kidnapping for ransom, hostage‑taking or hijacking;  
(ee) use of explosives by any device, or possession of any explosive substance without lawful justification;  
(f) inciting hatred and contempt on religious, sectarian or ethnic basis to stir up violence or cause internal disturbance;  
(g) taking the law into one’s own hands, awarding any punishment by an organization, individual or group not recognised by law, with a view to coerce, intimidate or terrorise the public, individuals, groups, communities, government officials and institutions, including law‑enforcement agencies;  
(h) firing on religious congregations, places of worship or random firing to spread panic, or forcible takeover of such places;  
(i) creating a serious risk to the safety of the public or a section of the public, or designing actions to frighten the public and prevent them from carrying out lawful trade and daily business, thereby disrupting civic life;  
(j) burning vehicles or any other serious form of arson;  
(k) extortion of money (“bhatta”) or property;  
(l) seriously interfering with or disrupting a communication system or public utility service;  
(m) serious coercion or intimidation of a public servant to force him to discharge or refrain from discharging his lawful duties;  
(n) serious violence against a member of the police force, armed forces, civil armed forces, or a public servant;  
(o) acts as part of armed resistance by groups or individuals against law‑enforcement agencies;  
(p) dissemination, preaching or broadcasting ideas, teachings or beliefs on FM stations or any other means of communication without explicit approval of the government or its concerned departments. [Source 1]

**Section 6(3) – Weapon‑related acts**  
The use or threat of any action listed in sub‑section (2) that involves firearms, explosives or any other weapon is terrorism, irrespective of whether sub‑section (1)(c) is satisfied. [Source 1]

**Section 6(3A) – Convention‑based acts**  
Notwithstanding anything in sub‑section (1), an action that violates a convention specified in the Fifth Schedule is an act of terrorism under this Act. [Source 1]

**Section 6(4) – Scope of “action”**  
In this section, “action” includes an act or a series of acts. [Source 1]

**Section 6(5) – Benefit of proscribed organisations**  
Terrorism includes any act done for the benefit of a proscribed organisation. [Source 1]

**Section 6(6) – Liability**  
A person who commits an offence under this section or any other provision of this Act shall be guilty of an act of terrorism. [Source 1]

**Section 6(7) – Definition of “terrorist”**  
A “terrorist” means:  

(a) any person who has committed an offence of terrorism under this Act and is or has been concerned in the commission, preparation, facilitation, funding or instigation of acts of terrorism; and  

(b) any person who, whether before or after the coming into force of this Act, is or has been concerned in the commission, preparation, facilitation, funding or instigation of acts of terrorism. [Source 1]
